 "old_string": "Further confirmed: on a 14x6 grid with background 3 and marker 7, strict horizontal `7 3 7` pairs at row 7 and row 11 (1-indexed), columns 2–4, had only their middle 3s recolored to 2; all other cells stayed unchanged. A 27x15 test with background 9 and marker 8 was solved on first submission by changing exactly the center cell in every horizontal `8 9 8` triplet to 2, including overlapping/repeated patterns such as `8 9 8 9 8` (both centers become 2); no vertical filling. This reinforces per-row local, overlapping-match evaluation across recurring variants. The marker/background pair is inferred per instance; fill color is 2.",
 "new_string": "Further confirmed: on a 14x6 grid with background 3 and marker 7, strict horizontal `7 3 7` pairs at row 7 and row 11 (1-indexed), columns 2–4, had only their middle 3s recolored to 2; all other cells stayed unchanged. A 27x15 test with background 9 and marker 8 was solved on first submission by changing exactly the center cell in every horizontal `8 9 8` triplet to 2, including overlapping/repeated patterns such as `8 9 8 9 8` (both centers become 2); no vertical filling. This reinforces per-row local, overlapping-match evaluation across recurring variants. The marker/background pair is inferred per instance; fill color is 2.\\n\\nAdditional demo: 18x21 with background 9 and marker 3. Output replaces every 9 in each uninterrupted horizontal alternating run `3 9 3 9 3 ...` with 2, including terminal gaps that have matching markers on both sides. Isolated sequences such as `3 9 3` were similarly filled; all cells outside the alternating horizontal marker runs remained unchanged. This is consistent with detecting each strict `marker, background, marker` triple independently, including overlapping matches, not a broad component- or vertical-based fill.\\n\\nCaution from one failed attempt: On a 25x7 test with background 0 and marker 1, filling the middle cell of every apparent horizontal `1 0 1` triple was wrong; request a demo rather than assuming that the local triple rule generalizes when this task id recurs. The demo established the strict alternating-run behavior on a different palette, but does not reveal the intended rule for that failed 25x7 input specifically. After seeing that demo, the same local triple fill for `1 0 1` was accepted on the test (the row-12 pattern `1 0 1 0` had only the single complete triple filled; incomplete terminal gap left alone). Wrong feedback should therefore prompt demo-based hypothesis revision, while recognizing accepted case may still validate local matching with palette-specific context."
